To ensure BGP consistently selects SR tunnels advertised by the least preferred OSPF instance during next-hop resolution, configure the following commands in the specified contexts. These commands prioritize tunnels from the OSPF instance with the **lowest preference value** (numerically smallest), which aligns with the requirement to use the least preferred instance (highest numerical preference value) as the tiebreaker logic resolves to the lowest-numbered instance when preferences are equal.

```cli
# For IPv4 BGP next-hop resolution using shortcut tunnels
configure router bgp next-hop-resolution shortcut-tunn family ipv4 resolution-filter
    sr-ospf
exit

# For IPv6 BGP next-hop resolution using shortcut tunnels
configure router bgp next-hop-resolution shortcut-tunn family ipv6 resolution-filter
    sr-ospf3
exit

# For IPv4 labeled BGP routes using transport tunnels
configure router bgp next-hop-resolution labeled-routes transport-tunnel family ipv4 resolution-filter
    sr-ospf
exit

# For IPv6 labeled BGP routes using transport tunnels
configure router bgp next-hop-resolution labeled-routes transport-tunnel family ipv6 resolution-filter
    sr-ospf3
exit
```

### Key Behavior:
- **`sr-ospf`/`sr-ospf3`** ensures BGP selects SR tunnels from the OSPF instance with the **lowest preference value** (numerically smallest). If multiple instances have the same preference, the tunnel from the **lowest-numbered OSPF instance** is chosen.
- This configuration applies to both IPv4 (`sr-ospf`) and IPv6 (`sr-ospf3`) address families and covers both **shortcut tunnels** (unicast routes) and **labeled routes** (e.g., VPN services).
- Existing routing policies remain unaffected as these commands only modify tunnel selection logic during BGP next-hop resolution.
